<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yazathingyan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1312</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yazathingyan was the second of the three brothers who founded the Myinsaing regime in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in the late 13th century. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> he governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> district, overthrew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyawswa-of-pagan">Kyawswa of Pagan</a> in 1297 and withstood a Yuan Mongol army in 1300 to 1301. After his death, around 1312, Thihathu ruled alone and founded the Pinya Kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yazathingyan was born around 1263 into a prominent family of Myinsaing in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, the second of four children. His father, Theinkha Bo, was a younger brother of the sawbwa, or chief, of Binnaka and had fled to Myinsaing after a quarrel with his brother in 1260; there he married the daughter of a wealthy banker. Scholarship of the British colonial era called the father a Shan, but the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-aung-thwin">Michael Aung-Thwin</a> has rejected the claim for want of any evidence.</p><p>Following his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a>, Yazathingyan entered the service of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> of Pagan, and his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> joined them. The three distinguished themselves in the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> that began in 1277, and from 1285, still in their twenties, they led the defence of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> while the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>. The names by which they are known were probably official titles given to them at this time. After the king was assassinated on 1 July 1287, the brothers held back a Mongol advance from Tagaung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the anarchy that followed, the brothers took over the army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> region, the main granary of the kingdom. King Kyawswa, who held little beyond his capital, named them viceroys on 19 February 1293, Yazathingyan receiving Mekkhaya, but their inscription of the appointment declared them the king's equals. When Kyawswa sought Mongol protection and was recognised by the emperor Temür Khan in March 1297, the brothers deposed him on 17 December 1297 with the help of the dowager queen Pwa Saw and set up his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-hnit">Saw Hnit</a> as a puppet king, ruling as co-regents. Kyawswa and his eldest son Theingapati were executed in May 1299.</p><p>In January 1300 the brothers attacked the Mongol garrisons at Singu and Male. A Mongol army of 12,000 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> besieged Myinsaing from 25 January 1301, and its main assault on 28 February failed. In April it withdrew after the brothers paid 800 taels of gold and 2,200 taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> took a royal title in 1306 and proclaimed himself king on 20 October 1309, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a> died on 13 April 1310. The main chronicles say that Yazathingyan then passed into the background and died in 1312 or 1313, but a stone inscription of 1329 states that he succeeded to the throne as Athinkhaya II before Thihathu. Thihathu founded the Pinya Kingdom on 7 February 1313, claiming to succeed the Pagan dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yazathingyan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athinkhaya, <b>Yazathingyan</b> and Thihathu, sons of a Shan chief and commanders under the last Pagan kings, built up power in the Kyaukse district from bases at Myinsaing, Mekkaya and Pinle.</blockquote><a href="/state/myinsaing-kingdom">Myinsaing Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyaukse District, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Mekkhaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.75,96.25&amp;year=1302" aria-label="Kyaukse District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7132 1360 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yazathingyan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7582 1630h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7582 1630h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.75,96.25&amp;year=1302">What was here in 1302? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
